# Performance

Canonical: `docs/architecture-plan.md` — the perf section.

## Request path

No blocking I/O in domain or adapters. Use async drivers for DB, cache and
HTTP. If a library is sync-only, isolate at **infra** with a bounded thread
pool — never in a use-case. Explicit timeouts on every egress (realtime SDK,
object storage, link previews, webhooks).

## Queries

- Tenant-scoped lists: **always** constrain the tenant key.
- Cursor pagination — no `OFFSET` on hot lists.
- No N+1: directories and per-row lookups use a projection/cache, not a
  query per row.
- Lean DTOs; never a `to_dict()` dump of ORM graphs.
- Index changes go in the migration tool, not "we'll add it later."

## Realtime

- The API role does not hold sockets; the gateway role does. When running
  combined, still call the same domain write.
- Fan-out: outbox row in the **same transaction**, then per-channel /
  per-user publish — not one global pubsub.
- Private channels: entitled sets only. Never broadcast a private message
  to the whole tenant.
- Presence = cache TTL only. No process-local dict.
- Backpressure: max payload, max subs, idle timeout; drop slow clients.

## Payloads and UI

- Heavy editors/SDKs deferred — not on the boot graph.
- Permission snapshot before painting privileged controls.
- Incremental lists; never load whole histories.

## Jobs

Background work is a queued **job**, not `threading.Thread`. Scheduler runs
exactly one replica. Do not hold a DB transaction across external calls.

## Smell test

If the shape is sync-framework + thread-per-connection + ad-hoc workers on
the primary DB, it is wrong for this stack.
